{
  "concept": "In a right triangle, the side labels adjacent, opposite, and hypotenuse are roles defined relative to the chosen acute angle, and those roles determine which inverse trigonometric function recovers the angle from known side lengths.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "plain black right triangle outline",
    "horizontal base side labeled a beneath its midpoint",
    "vertical right side labeled p along its outer edge",
    "slanted hypotenuse labeled h above its midpoint",
    "left acute angle arc labeled theta",
    "small open square marking the right angle at the lower right vertex",
    "left vertex where theta is located",
    "lower right vertex joining sides a and p",
    "upper right vertex joining sides p and h",
    "selected-side highlight overlay for a, p, or h",
    "theta highlight overlay that appears with selected side",
    "role callout label reading adjacent, opposite, or hypotenuse",
    "inverse trig relation callout showing theta = sin^-1(p/h), theta = cos^-1(a/h), or theta = tan^-1(p/a)",
    "subtle dashed guide showing changing height p as theta changes",
    "subtle dashed guide showing changing base a as theta changes"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "sideRoleClick",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "A side's name is not just its position on the page: a is adjacent to theta, p is opposite theta, and h is always the hypotenuse because those roles are defined relative to the marked angle."
    },
    {
      "id": "thetaSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle theta",
      "range": [
        10,
        80,
        1
      ],
      "default": 35,
      "teaches": "As theta increases, the opposite side grows relative to the adjacent side, so the ratios p/h, a/h, and p/a change in predictable trigonometric ways."
    },
    {
      "id": "ratioInput",
      "type": "equation_input",
      "label": "Known side ratio",
      "fields": [
        {
          "id": "knownRatio",
          "label": "ratio =",
          "default": "p/h",
          "variables": [
            "a",
            "p",
            "h"
          ],
          "domain": [
            0,
            1
          ],
          "role": "inverse_trig_ratio"
        }
      ],
      "teaches": "The known pair of side lengths determines the inverse trig relation: p/h points to sine, a/h points to cosine, and p/a points to tangent."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Identify the reference angle",
      "narration": "The marked angle theta at the left vertex is the reference point for naming the sides. The labels a, p, and h describe each side's role relative to theta, not merely its direction on the page.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSlider": 35
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Click each side role",
      "narration": "Click a side to highlight it with theta: a is adjacent because it touches theta, p is opposite because it faces theta, and h is the hypotenuse because it lies across from the right angle.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSlider": 35
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change theta",
      "narration": "Drag theta wider and narrower. The triangle keeps a right angle, but the balance between opposite side p and adjacent side a changes continuously.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSlider": 60
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Choose the inverse trig relation",
      "narration": "Enter a known ratio such as p/h, a/h, or p/a. The matching inverse relation is highlighted, showing which formula recovers the unknown angle theta.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSlider": 35
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Once the sides are named relative to theta, the side pair you know tells you exactly which inverse trig function to use.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the figure as a clean black-outline right triangle with no axes, grid, numbers, or shading. Place the base horizontally from left to right, the vertical side rising at the right end, and the hypotenuse slanting upward from the left vertex to the top of the vertical side. Put the right-angle square at the lower right corner and the theta arc at the left vertex. Keep labels in simple italic math style: a below the base, p just outside the vertical side, h above the hypotenuse, and theta beside the left angle arc. For the slider, hold the hypotenuse length visually constant while theta changes, so a shortens and p lengthens as theta increases. Use a warm highlight color for the selected side and theta arc, and show the inverse trig callout near the triangle without cluttering the original minimal diagram."
}

